## Part 4: Google Colab T4 Training Specification

### Hardware Constraints
| Resource | T4 Free Tier | T4 Pro |
|---|---|---|
| GPU VRAM | 15GB | 15GB |
| System RAM | 12.7GB | 51GB |
| Disk | 78GB | 166GB |
| Runtime | 4-12 hours | 24 hours |

### Memory Budget for QLoRA Training

```
Base Model (Phi-3.5-mini, 4-bit NF4):     ~2.1 GB
LoRA Adapters (rank 16, alpha 32):          ~0.1 GB  
Optimizer States (8-bit AdamW):             ~0.2 GB
Gradient Checkpointing Activations:         ~2.0 GB
Input Batch (seq_len=2048, batch=2):        ~0.5 GB
FAISS Index + Embedder:                     ~0.4 GB
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TOTAL:                                      ~5.3 GB ✅ (fits in 15GB T4)
```

### Training Hyperparameters

```python
training_config = {
    # Model
    "base_model": "microsoft/Phi-3.5-mini-instruct",
    "quantization": "nf4",           # 4-bit NormalFloat
    "compute_dtype": "bfloat16",
    
    # LoRA
    "lora_r": 16,                    # Rank
    "lora_alpha": 32,                # Scaling factor
    "lora_dropout": 0.05,
    "target_modules": ["q_proj", "k_proj", "v_proj", "o_proj",
                        "gate_proj", "up_proj", "down_proj"],
    
    # Training
    "epochs": 3,
    "batch_size": 2,
    "gradient_accumulation_steps": 8,  # Effective batch = 16
    "learning_rate": 2e-4,
    "lr_scheduler": "cosine",
    "warmup_ratio": 0.03,
    "max_seq_length": 2048,
    "gradient_checkpointing": True,
    
    # Optimization
    "optim": "paged_adamw_8bit",     # Memory-efficient optimizer
    "fp16": False,
    "bf16": True,
    
    # Saving
    "save_steps": 100,
    "logging_steps": 10,
    "output_dir": "./paradox_qlora_checkpoints",
}
```

### Training Data Format

Transform your existing brain shards into instruction-tuning format:

```json
{
  "instruction": "Explain the concept of game theory and its applications.",
  "input": "CONTEXT: Game theory is the mathematical study of strategic interactions...",
  "output": "Game theory provides the mathematical framework for analyzing decisions where the outcome depends on the choices of multiple agents. At its core, it models rational actors navigating interdependent decision spaces...",
  "region": "PFC",
  "metadata": {"source": "brain_shard", "topic": "Game_theory"}
}
```

